ain that  had divided europe for decades  [Music]  it was gorbachev's reformist policies  that helped unleash a peaceful  revolution across eastern europe  [Music]  in 1990 gorbachev was awarded the nobel  peace prize for his key role in bringing  about a new peaceful world order  internationally he was highly regarded  but at home he was increasingly under  pressure the economic situation was dire  the soviet union on the verge of falling  apart  communist hardliners staged a coup  against gorbachev in august 1991.  at the end of that year mikhail  gorbachev announced his resignation and  the soviet union was dissolved with many  russians holding him responsible for its  demise  [Music]  well the passing of mikhail gorbachev  has triggered across the globe waves of  emotions and reflections and they all  speak to the impact that gorbachev and  his policies had far beyond the borders  of the former soviet union  joining me tonight to discuss the legacy  are two authorities on soviet and  russian history as well as foreign  policy angela stent she's the author of  putin's world  russia against the west and with the  rest  professor stint is a senior fellow at  the brookings institution she joins me  tonight from washington and i'm also  happy to welcome to the program william  taubman  he is a pulitzer prize-winning author  his latest book is entitled gorbachev  his life and times and mr talbon is also  in the u.s capitol tonight to both of  you welcome to the program i'm glad that  you could take the time  with us this evening to talk about the  life and legacy of mikhail gorbachev  his death became known public knowledge  about 24 hours ago the west  has been emoting ever since about a  great statesman  inside russia  we've had a telegram from the kremlin  sent to the family and that's about it  we in the west it seems wish that we had  had more gorbachev and in the kremlin  you get the sense that they are wishing  that they had never had any gorbachev  professor stint let me start with you um  what do you say to that is that how the  the dividing lines are tonight  well they currently are certainly like  that  in western europe and the united states  we think gorbachev is a great statesman  who ended the cold war allowed germany  to unify  peacefully and really liberated millions  of people inside the soviet union  freeing political prisoners and then in  eastern europe who had been part of the  soviet bloc um that's not how he's seen  clearly either in the kremlin or among  many russians uh who  bemoan the fall of the soviet union  after all putin himself has said that  the collapse of the soviet union was the  greatest geopolitical catastrophe of the  20th century because it left millions of  russians outside of russia and he's  right now trying to remedy it and really  he has systematically gone about  reversing many of the reforms that  gorbachev himself did  yeah we're going to talk about that in  just a moment mr talbon let me  ask you about the notion of gorbachev  inside russia you have written that  gorbachev was  too decent  to be the leader of his  country why do you think that is  well just look at his predecessors uh  soviet leaders like lenin stalin  they were  all too  ready to shed blood in the case of  stalin especially on a mammoth scale  to attain their aims gorbachev was  reluctant to use force and violence  gorbachev  was very optimistic about the  possibilities of people coming to  govern themselves in a democracy  gorbachev trusted reagan thatcher  mitterrand coal to work with him to end  the cold war  these qualities of optimism of trust  of revulsion against force and violence  these  turned out to  not be  sufficiently  suitable to a country in which had  experienced so much force and violence  so much  totalitarianism  i got to know him fairly well when i was  interviewing him for the book and in  person he struck us too as a as a decent  man warm informal  uh natural with a sense of humor  uh i often wondered how the hell he had  managed to  defeat his rivals to become the leader  of this country and then how he dared to  think that he could get away with  transforming it and the world in the  ways that he dreamed he could well mr  talbot  that's that's a good point you make  gorbachev he set into motion forces that  he would later not be able to steer or  control at all i mean  and we have to remember he was a  communist leader who wanted reform but  there was no guidebook for him to turn a  command economy into something that  resembled a market economy i'm wondering  if if you think did he push forward with  these reforms somewhat blindly and  with a considerable degree of naivete  well i would say yes he was naive and  even utopian in a sense that he tried to  do  what turned out to be impossible you can  make a pretty good case  that his whole project was doomed from  the start by  russia what it was by its own history by  the forces who remained from the soviet  totalitarianism  who were allied in a raid against him  but nonetheless he tried  and in that sense you know one has to  respect him admire him  and almost wish for more  leaders in other countries who would  dare to try  and professor stint  gorbachev of course um was not he wasn't  a simple man he was complex he was  complicated he was a complex and  complicated  russian  he considered the annexation of crimea  to be correct  in 2014  but he was quoted as saying that war  with ukraine would never happen he said  also even if you can't prove it  scientifically  the russians and the ukrainians they  belong  together  what picture does that paint then of  gorbachev well i think his attitudes  were very much those of soviet people  and many russians today uh after all  ukraine you know was part of the soviet  union um ukraine and russia have a  history a very complicated history that  goes back centuries and so  most russians found it very hard when  the soviet union collapsed to accept  that ukrainians were a separate nation  uh and had the right to a separate state  so i i think it's uh unremarkable that  he said those things but we should also  remember that he unleashed a process  and and i think when gorbachev came to  the united states in the early 90s and  he was speaking at the library of  congress somebody asked him what was  your biggest mistake and he said in an  understatement i underestimated the  nationalities question so i think like  many soviet uh leaders and soviet  citizens he didn't realize how much the  non-russian nationalities actually  wanted their own independence and as  soon as he opened up glasnost and people  were able to discuss these issues their  desire for freedom and independence  became stronger but he did unleash a  process where the ukrainians were while  the soviet union still existed were able  to elect their own leader within the  soviet union and so again these were  processes that he unleashed that he  didn't fully understand and that he  didn't intend to do at the beginning he  did not intend to see the destruction of  the soviet union but we have to remember  that he in the end did nothing to stop  it um and i think that again he's unlike  most russian leaders that he voluntarily  stepped back admittedly under pressure  from boris yeltsin and the ukrainian and  bella russian leaders but he didn't  fight to stay in power he didn't have  the army come and support him so um  i think we shouldn't criticize him too  much for what he said about ukraine  because for a man of his generation um  it's not surprising professor i want to  pick up on the on the putin factor here  you along with fiona hill um  wrote an article in foreign affairs  recently and the title is the world  putin wants  has  putin succeeded already in dismantling  everything that gorbachev was able to  achieve  inside russia in terms of greater  openness  and  extending freedom such as freedom of  expression freedom of the press  well russia is a far more oppressive  place now than it was uh the soviet  union was under gorbachev um and it  resembles to some extent what life was  like under the region of p in the  brethren period the only difference is  that russians can still with greater  difficulty leave russia he really has uh  dismantled most of it and primarily he  is hell-bent on subjugating ukraine on  reabsorbing ukraine into russia into  some kind of a slavic federation so he  is deliberately reversing  in essence the collapse of the soviet  union he said that the collapse of the  soviet union was the greatest  geopolitical catastrophe and he's  determined to reverse that but whether  he can do it i don't know but it's  certainly the direct opposite of  everything that gorbachev said in motion  and mr chapman do you see then the the  invasion of ukraine is is this  vladimir putin's  remedy to  the disaster that occurred  during gorbachev's watch  well putin obviously thinks so i i don't  think so myself because it's hard to  characterize a bloodbath uh with all  kinds of horrendous consequences  as a remedy  but he cert this is putin's way of  saying  uh without saying it that gorbachev was  responsible for allowing the soviet  union to fall apart and and that falling  apart included as angela just said  the the  attempt  successful in the end by so many of  those soviet republics to break away and  become independent and sovereign and try  to determine their own future  i'd like to get  both of you to give me your opinions on  on this assessment there are some  americans who may be tempted to call  mikhail gorbachev the soviet jimmy  carter  a leader a man who had the best  intentions  for his country and his people but whose  policies left voters feeling worse not  better mr tubman what do you say  was he was he the soviet jimmy carter at  the end of the day  i'm sorry were you asking me yes yes  yes  well  i'm rolling that around in my head  as i said there was a certain naivete to  gorbachev there was a certain utopianism  but  on the other hand gorbachev was tough  and canny and his ability  working within the still  confines of the soviet system to get  commerce communist hardliners to give up  their power  until they changed their mind and tried  to get his  uh uh get him to give up his was really  quite extraordinary when you go back to  1985  when he took over  and assumed the leadership of a  politburo that included  the brezhnev cohorts cohorts and lackeys  it's really remarkable what he was able  to do in fact the irony i think  is that he proved to be a more  successful  ruthless manipulator of the old levers  of the old soviet system  than a uh  a sort of entrepreneur of democracy he  was better at forcing out germico  the kremlin  than in  mobilizing electoral forces to back his  policies once he had opened up  the system to free  voting  elections and  created a genuinely  functioning parliament  and professor  what's your take on this because  after he left politics gorbachev you  know he set up this institute that was  trying to help civil society in russia i  mean there are analogies to what we've  seen  with former president jimmy carter but  other u.s presidents  do you see americans maybe being able to  draw this comparison  well with all due respect to president  carter um i think what president uh  leader gorbachev did um was far more  consequential because even though he  made mistakes uh he clearly vacillated  between economic reform and then going  back to the centrally planned system um  he he was a very good tactician but in  the end i think he didn't have a very  good strategy given all of that  freeing the russian people giving them a  greater liberty and freedom of  expression that outlasted him it  certainly lasted into the 90s we can  discuss what else happened in the 90s  but this this feeling of greater  empowerment that he gave russians that  outlasted him this is something now  obviously that the putin regime is  trying to crush and to try and reverse  but even younger people today and today  you can follow the tweets that alexey  navalny the opposition leader who is in  solitary confinement in prison but  somehow gets the tweets out through his  lawyers um  praising gorbachev not only for these  freedoms but because he's an example of  a leader who wasn't corrupt he could  have accumulated wealth and all the  finer things of life and he didn't so i  think he set an example there which even  though uh it's very difficult to follow  it at the moment in russia is something  that has outlasted him and that even  younger people understand and professor  stan i'd like maybe if you could help me  here and try to connect the legacy of  gorbachev to the situation we see in  modern day america tomorrow evening u.s  president biden plans to speak to the  american people reportedly  about the battle for the soul of the  country he's concerned about that  is there a connection to the death of  gorbachev gorbachev represented ideas  that made  our current world order possible he has  now passed away  and  do you do a lot of americans feel that  the world that as we know it also seems  to be slipping away as well  well certainly gorbachev promoted  successfully greater democracy uh within  the soviet union and what we see today  in the united states clearly is an  erosion of democracy um and and and  if not half of the country significant  part of the country no longer believing  in normal democratic norms and the rule  of law so i think it's a very it's a  very serious situation um and i think uh  it behooves us to look at other  countries  where where these freedoms and the rule  of law no longer exist and remind  ourselves that if we go down that path  then in fact that will be the end of  this democratic experiment in america  we've got two more minutes i've got a  final question to you both mr chapman  i'll start with you gorbachev and putin  never criticized each other  publicly at least not directly putin did  not praise gorbachev today  but he also did not cast him in a  negative light even in that telegram  that was sent to the gorbachev family  does gorbachev in your opinion does his  legacy does it present  any type of threat to putin more  in depth than gorbachev did in life  well you know among the apparently tens  of thousands maybe hundreds of thousands  of russians who have immigrated fled  from putin's russia after the war and  ukraine began  at least one person was telling me the  other day  that there are a lot of russians who  believe another perestroika  is necessary  now i can readily believe that among  those who have fled from putin's russia  i guess the question is are there among  those who have not fled especially the  younger generation  is is it dawning on them that the kind  of reforms that  that gorbachev favored the peristroika  uh is again  something that they hope to see  happening in russia i guess i'm  skeptical as to how wide that sentiment  is after all these years in which  russians have accustomed themselves to  uh despising russia gorbachev  unfortunately we're out of time but i  want to thank both of you angela stint  senior fellow at the brookings  institution and william taubman pulitzer  prize-winning author to both of you we  appreciate your time and your insights  an excellent discussion helping us  understand the legacy of mikhail  gorbachev thank you  thank you  you

