h and to try and reverse  but even younger people today and today  you can follow the tweets that alexey  navalny the opposition leader who is in  solitary confinement in prison but  somehow gets the tweets out through his  lawyers um  praising gorbachev not only for these  freedoms but because he's an example of  a leader who wasn't corrupt he could  have accumulated wealth and all the  finer things of life and he didn't so i  think he set an example there which even  though uh it's very difficult to follow  it at the moment in russia is something  that has outlasted him and that even  younger people understand and professor  stan i'd like maybe if you could help me  here and try to connect the legacy of  gorbachev to the situation we see in  modern day america tomorrow evening u.s  president biden plans to speak to the  american people reportedly  about the battle for the soul of the  country he's concerned about that  is there a connection to the death of  gorbachev gorbachev represented ideas  that made  our current world order possible he has  now passed away  and  do you do a lot of americans feel that  the world that as we know it also seems  to be slipping away as well  well certainly gorbachev promoted  successfully greater democracy uh within  the soviet union and what we see today  in the united states clearly is an  erosion of democracy um and and and  if not half of the country significant  part of the country no longer believing  in normal democratic norms and the rule  of law so i think it's a very it's a  very serious situation um and i think uh  it behooves us to look at other  countries  where where these freedoms and the rule  of law no longer exist and remind  ourselves that if we go down that path  then in fact that will be the end of  this democratic experiment in america  we've got two more minutes i've got a  final question to you both mr chapman  i'll start with you gorbachev and putin  never criticized each other  publicly at least not directly putin did  not praise gorbachev today  but he also did not cast him in a  negative light even in that telegram  that was sent to the gorbachev family  does gorbachev in your opinion does his  legacy does it present  any type of threat to putin more  in depth than gorbachev did in life  well you know among the apparently tens  of thousands maybe hundreds of thousands  of russians who have immigrated fled  from putin's russia after the war and  ukraine began  at least one person was telling me the  other day  that there are a lot of russians who  believe another perestroika  is necessary  now i can readily believe that among  those who have fled from putin's russia  i guess the question is are there among  those who have not fled especially the  younger generation  is is it dawning on them that the kind  of reforms that  that gorbachev favored the peristroika  uh is again  something that they hope to see  happening in russia i guess i'm  skeptical as to how wide that sentiment  is after all these years in which  russians have accustomed themselves to  uh despising russia gorbachev  unfortunately we're out of time but i  want to thank both of you angela stint  senior fellow at the brookings  institution and william taubman pulitzer  prize-winning author to both of you we  appreciate your time and your insights  an excellent discussion helping us  understand the legacy of mikhail  gorbachev thank you  thank you  you
 

